3185878,0.9969960497125939,0.9973286765575047,0.9977735535084801,0.9982558471614773,0.9987506130484819,0.999249536346822,0.9994161784486071,0.999138058815535,0.9990453522711776,0.9986814504233922,0.9982271501411306,0.9980757167137102,0.9980252389045701,0.998012619452285],"years":[2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024]}},"statement0":"<p style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\">The word <span  \"><i>\"dedication\" </i></span> is mentioned two or more times in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0.1%</span> of psychology papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. </p>Among 2020-2024 papers containing <span  \"><i>\"dedication\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">19.4%</span> of reported significant p-values (p < .05) are fragile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a lower than the typical Psychology paper (median shown for reference); relative to other words, papers with <span  \"><i>\"dedication\" </i></span> are in the bottom <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">1<sup>st</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages (lower = better). Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability. Among papers where under 32% of significant p-values are fragile, the rate of successful replication was found to be 59.5%, whereas for papers over the replication rate was just 33.8% (see <a href=\"../assets/pdf/Bogdan_2025_PsyChange_Manuscript_SuppMat.pdf\">Supplemental Materials 7.3 of the mentioned paper</a>).","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span  \"><i>\"dedication\" </i></span> tend to be published in higher impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">62<sup>nd</sup></span> percentile among all words)  and tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">99<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are less likely to publish papers containing <span  \"><i>\"dedication\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0<sup>th</sup></span> percentile). "}